  "slug": "photonic-edge-025e",
  "summary": "Recent preprints report exact mobility edges in a 2D photonic quasicrystal and nonlocality requirements for accidental BICs in metasurfaces.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the control of mode localization and confinement in engineered wave systems, where flat bands, bound states in the continuum and mobility edges determine which modes are trapped. The experimental preprint on a two-dimensional photonic quasicrystal reports exact mobility edges and flat-band states realized with auxiliary subsystems and photonic waveguides. The metasurface study models bound states in the continuum through converging reflection and transmission zeros and argues that accidental BICs require nonlocal, spatially extended coupling. The acoustic preprint demonstrates BICs in a 3D-printed labyrinthine meta-atom within a waveguide, with symmetry breaking central to the resulting states. The non-Hermitian tight-binding study addresses a different question: a single nonlinear phase shift on boundary sites restores extended modes in a 1D model exhibiting the skin effect. The new element across these items is the explicit use of nonlocality, symmetry breaking and boundary nonlinearity as handles on mode localization.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
